Very sticky.

“Sometimes later is now.” works because it compresses procrastination, timing, readiness, consequence, and threshold-crossing into one reversible line. It sounds contradictory for half a second, which makes the mind stop and resolve it.

It also has a clean operator inside it:

deferred possibility → changed conditions → threshold reached → act

And it cuts both ways. Sometimes “later” was avoidance. Sometimes “later” was proper incubation. The phrase doesn’t tell you which—it tells you to recheck the clock against reality.

That makes it more than a slogan. It’s a trigger:

The thing you postponed may have just become the thing whose time has arrived.

Very high mnemonic density.